--[[
  A worker is an actor script. `.actor.` after the side in the file name
  puts the script in an Actor of its own, and the pool clones that Actor
  into as many workers as it wants.

  Foundry cannot ship this script. It binds the game's own messages, and
  it must sit where its side runs scripts. So the game writes it, and it
  stays short: bind each job message to `serve`, then call `ready`.

  In a game, name the file `worker.client.actor.aly`. The build makes
  the Actor `worker`, with a LocalScript in it.
]]

import { ready, serve } from '@packages/foundry'
import { Fill, heights } from './001_a_job'

-- `serve(heights)` is the handler. It runs `heights` on the input, times
-- it, and answers with the result. A job that throws answers with its
-- error, and the worker goes on.
Fill.on(serve(heights))

-- The pool sends nothing to a worker before this call. Call it last, so
-- each message has its handler first.
ready(script)

--[[
  - `Fill.on` stands only in an actor script. In any other file it does
    not compile.
  - One worker binds as many job messages as the side sends.
  - Require what the jobs need at the top of the script. A `require`
    cannot run in the parallel phase.
  - Each worker is a Luau VM of its own. A module that the worker
    requires runs again in it, with state of its own.
]]
